			<!-- UAMSWP Output Side-by-Side Image & Text -->
						<script>var json_data = [{"ID":173254,"date":"2026-09-04T08:14:51","link":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/","modified":"2026-09-04T08:14:51","title":"Myeloma Patient, Family Credit UAMS for His Improved Outlook","content":"<p>Chris Seab has come a long way since his myeloma diagnosis, and the future looks better every day.<\/p>\n<p>On a recent visit with his wife Liz to the Winthrop P. Rockefeller Cancer Institute\u2019s Myeloma Center at the University of Arkansas for Medical Sciences (UAMS), the Seabs, who live in Billings, Montana, talked about Seab\u2019s progress.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOur visit here last summer was very positive, and this recent trip was even better,\u201d he said. \u201cMy numbers have continued to drop.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>An injury to his back in March 2024 while skiing turned out to be the first sign of a problem.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cIt felt like a bad muscle pull,\u201d Chris Seab said. \u201cI went to a chiropractor, but it didn\u2019t help. Acupuncture was a little better, but not much.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>The issue escalated on a business trip.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOne day I was walking to my hotel, and it felt like if I took one more step I\u2019d fall over \u2014 the pain was so intense,\u201d he said. \u201cI spent the next couple of days in bed before I was able to drive home.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>\u201cMemorial Day weekend I was working at home in our kitchen and my pen fell to the floor,\u201d he continued. \u201cI went to pick it up, fell out of the chair, and I couldn\u2019t get back up. I called my wife, and she and my son managed to get me to the emergency room.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>Tests revealed he had shattered the L5 vertebrae in his lower back, and he likely had cancer. Additionally, Seab had lesions all over his body, including the legs, pelvis, shoulder, and spine.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cAn orthopaedic surgeon thought they may need to put a rod in my left leg,\u201d he said.<\/p>\n<p>Liz Seab started calling around for recommendations and &#8220;Go to UAMS,&#8221; was the most common response.<\/p>\n<p>Chris Seab\u2019s first appointment was in June 2024, when he was assigned to Myeloma Center physician Maurizio Zangari, M.D.<\/p>\n<p>More testing at UAMS revealed the seriousness of his condition.<\/p>\n<div id=\"attachment_173258\" class=\"wp-caption alignright\" style=\"width: 210px\"><a href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-scaled.jpg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-173258 size-medium\" src=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-200x300.jpg\" alt=\"Rob Guba, Chris Seab, Burke Moran\" width=\"200\" height=\"300\" srcset=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-200x300.jpg 200w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-684x1024.jpg 684w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-768x1150.jpg 768w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1367x2048.jpg 1367w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-375x562.jpg 375w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-750x1124.jpg 750w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1140x1708.jpg 1140w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-scaled.jpg 1709w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px\" \/><\/a><p class=\"wp-caption-text\">Burke Moran (left) and Rob Guba (right), longtime friends of Chris Seab, led a cross-country bike ride in January and February 2025 that raised more than $30,000 for myeloma research at UAMS.<\/p><\/div>\n<p>\u201cHe had about 90% cancer in his bone marrow,\u201d Liz Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>Stem cell transplant was the prescribed treatment, with the first harvest of cells occurring in July 2024.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cI remember watching the Fourth of July fireworks out of my hospital room window,\u201d Chris Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>The first stem cell transplant took place in October 2024, followed by a second in February 2025. The second transplant was done on an outpatient basis.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cI enjoyed that a lot more,\u201d he said. \u201cI had a lot more freedom not being stuck in a hospital bed.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>While Seab was at UAMS for treatment last year, two of his childhood friends established the nonprofit Wheeling for Healing and embarked on a cross-county e-bike ride to raise money in support of him and others affected by multiple myeloma. Their effort resulted in $30,000 for myeloma research at UAMS.<\/p>\n<p>Liz Seab said the Stem Cell Transplant Unit provided excellent care for her husband.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cThey explained everything as we went along. We felt comfortable that anything that came up could be managed,\u201d she said.<\/p>\n<p>Following the transplants, Zangari prescribed a regimen beginning in April 2025 that included cancer drugs carfilzomib and isutaximab. This is when things began to steadily improve.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cThere was no leveling out with my results, the numbers gradually came down,\u201d Chris Saeb said.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cHe\u2019s doing very well,\u201d said Zangari said. \u201cWe\u2019re in a maintenance phase now.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>\u201cHis blood work looks really good,\u201d Liz Seab added. \u201cHe now goes for local treatment once a month.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>She also noted an unexpected result.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cChris\u2019 lesions are resolving themselves,\u201d she said. \u201cWe didn\u2019t realize that would be possible.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>More than two years after the initial diagnosis, the path looks clearer.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cEverything is positive,\u201d Chris Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOne of the greatest things we did was making the choice to come here,\u201d Liz Seab said. \u201cI think the trajectory of Chris\u2019 recovery would have been very different if we didn\u2019t have the benefit of the depth of experience and knowledge present at UAMS.\u201d<\/p>\n","excerpt":"<p>Chris Seab has come a long way since his myeloma diagnosis, and the future looks better every day.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","thumbnail":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-150x150.jpg","thumbalt":"Chris and Liz Seab","thumbcaption":"<p>Chris and Liz Seab of Billings, Montana. Chris Seab was initially diagnosed with myeloma in May 2024 and has received two stem cell transplants.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/uam_7277\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","image":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-1024x576.jpg","image_sm":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-512x288.jpg","imagealt":"Chris and Liz Seab","imagecaption":"<p>Chris and Liz Seab of Billings, Montana. Chris Seab was initially diagnosed with myeloma in May 2024 and has received two stem cell transplants.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/uam_7277\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","author_name":"Nathan Tidwell","terms":[{"term_id":13,"name":"Featured","slug":"featured","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":13,"taxonomy":"category","description":"","parent":0,"count":1327,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"},{"term_id":6894,"name":"Myeloma Center","slug":"myeloma-center","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":6894,"taxonomy":"category","description":"","parent":6892,"count":108,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"},{"term_id":29,"name":"Myeloma Patient Stories","slug":"myeloma","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":29,"taxonomy":"category","description":"Patient stories about Myeloma ","parent":18,"count":59,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"}]}];</script>			<section class="uamswp-news-syndication-wrapper uams-module bg-auto alignfull no-padding side-by-side image-on-left image-background-center img-fluid"id="side-by-side-173254" aria-label="Patient Stories">
				<div class="uamswp-news-syndication-side">
				<script>var json_data = [{"ID":173254,"date":"2026-09-04T08:14:51","link":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/","modified":"2026-09-04T08:14:51","title":"Myeloma Patient, Family Credit UAMS for His Improved Outlook","content":"<p>Chris Seab has come a long way since his myeloma diagnosis, and the future looks better every day.<\/p>\n<p>On a recent visit with his wife Liz to the Winthrop P. Rockefeller Cancer Institute\u2019s Myeloma Center at the University of Arkansas for Medical Sciences (UAMS), the Seabs, who live in Billings, Montana, talked about Seab\u2019s progress.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOur visit here last summer was very positive, and this recent trip was even better,\u201d he said. \u201cMy numbers have continued to drop.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>An injury to his back in March 2024 while skiing turned out to be the first sign of a problem.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cIt felt like a bad muscle pull,\u201d Chris Seab said. \u201cI went to a chiropractor, but it didn\u2019t help. Acupuncture was a little better, but not much.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>The issue escalated on a business trip.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOne day I was walking to my hotel, and it felt like if I took one more step I\u2019d fall over \u2014 the pain was so intense,\u201d he said. \u201cI spent the next couple of days in bed before I was able to drive home.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>\u201cMemorial Day weekend I was working at home in our kitchen and my pen fell to the floor,\u201d he continued. \u201cI went to pick it up, fell out of the chair, and I couldn\u2019t get back up. I called my wife, and she and my son managed to get me to the emergency room.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>Tests revealed he had shattered the L5 vertebrae in his lower back, and he likely had cancer. Additionally, Seab had lesions all over his body, including the legs, pelvis, shoulder, and spine.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cAn orthopaedic surgeon thought they may need to put a rod in my left leg,\u201d he said.<\/p>\n<p>Liz Seab started calling around for recommendations and &#8220;Go to UAMS,&#8221; was the most common response.<\/p>\n<p>Chris Seab\u2019s first appointment was in June 2024, when he was assigned to Myeloma Center physician Maurizio Zangari, M.D.<\/p>\n<p>More testing at UAMS revealed the seriousness of his condition.<\/p>\n<div id=\"attachment_173258\" class=\"wp-caption alignright\" style=\"width: 210px\"><a href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-scaled.jpg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-173258 size-medium\" src=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-200x300.jpg\" alt=\"Rob Guba, Chris Seab, Burke Moran\" width=\"200\" height=\"300\" srcset=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-200x300.jpg 200w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-684x1024.jpg 684w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-768x1150.jpg 768w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1367x2048.jpg 1367w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-375x562.jpg 375w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-750x1124.jpg 750w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-1140x1708.jpg 1140w, https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_8257-scaled.jpg 1709w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 200px) 100vw, 200px\" \/><\/a><p class=\"wp-caption-text\">Burke Moran (left) and Rob Guba (right), longtime friends of Chris Seab, led a cross-country bike ride in January and February 2025 that raised more than $30,000 for myeloma research at UAMS.<\/p><\/div>\n<p>\u201cHe had about 90% cancer in his bone marrow,\u201d Liz Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>Stem cell transplant was the prescribed treatment, with the first harvest of cells occurring in July 2024.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cI remember watching the Fourth of July fireworks out of my hospital room window,\u201d Chris Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>The first stem cell transplant took place in October 2024, followed by a second in February 2025. The second transplant was done on an outpatient basis.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cI enjoyed that a lot more,\u201d he said. \u201cI had a lot more freedom not being stuck in a hospital bed.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>While Seab was at UAMS for treatment last year, two of his childhood friends established the nonprofit Wheeling for Healing and embarked on a cross-county e-bike ride to raise money in support of him and others affected by multiple myeloma. Their effort resulted in $30,000 for myeloma research at UAMS.<\/p>\n<p>Liz Seab said the Stem Cell Transplant Unit provided excellent care for her husband.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cThey explained everything as we went along. We felt comfortable that anything that came up could be managed,\u201d she said.<\/p>\n<p>Following the transplants, Zangari prescribed a regimen beginning in April 2025 that included cancer drugs carfilzomib and isutaximab. This is when things began to steadily improve.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cThere was no leveling out with my results, the numbers gradually came down,\u201d Chris Saeb said.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cHe\u2019s doing very well,\u201d said Zangari said. \u201cWe\u2019re in a maintenance phase now.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>\u201cHis blood work looks really good,\u201d Liz Seab added. \u201cHe now goes for local treatment once a month.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>She also noted an unexpected result.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cChris\u2019 lesions are resolving themselves,\u201d she said. \u201cWe didn\u2019t realize that would be possible.\u201d<\/p>\n<p>More than two years after the initial diagnosis, the path looks clearer.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cEverything is positive,\u201d Chris Seab said.<\/p>\n<p>\u201cOne of the greatest things we did was making the choice to come here,\u201d Liz Seab said. \u201cI think the trajectory of Chris\u2019 recovery would have been very different if we didn\u2019t have the benefit of the depth of experience and knowledge present at UAMS.\u201d<\/p>\n","excerpt":"<p>Chris Seab has come a long way since his myeloma diagnosis, and the future looks better every day.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","thumbnail":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-150x150.jpg","thumbalt":"Chris and Liz Seab","thumbcaption":"<p>Chris and Liz Seab of Billings, Montana. Chris Seab was initially diagnosed with myeloma in May 2024 and has received two stem cell transplants.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/uam_7277\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","image":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-1024x576.jpg","image_sm":"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/09\/UAM_7277-512x288.jpg","imagealt":"Chris and Liz Seab","imagecaption":"<p>Chris and Liz Seab of Billings, Montana. Chris Seab was initially diagnosed with myeloma in May 2024 and has received two stem cell transplants.<\/p>\n<div><a class=\"more\" href=\"https:\/\/news.uams.edu\/2026\/09\/04\/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook\/uam_7277\/\">Read more<\/a><\/div>","author_name":"Nathan Tidwell","terms":[{"term_id":13,"name":"Featured","slug":"featured","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":13,"taxonomy":"category","description":"","parent":0,"count":1327,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"},{"term_id":6894,"name":"Myeloma Center","slug":"myeloma-center","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":6894,"taxonomy":"category","description":"","parent":6892,"count":108,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"},{"term_id":29,"name":"Myeloma Patient Stories","slug":"myeloma","term_group":0,"term_taxonomy_id":29,"taxonomy":"category","description":"Patient stories about Myeloma ","parent":18,"count":59,"filter":"raw","term_order":"0"}]}];</script>					<div class="container-fluid img-fluid">
						<div class="row">
							<div class="col-12 col-md-6 image-container" aria-label="Chris and Liz Seab" role="img">
								<style>
									#side-by-side-173254 .image-container { background-image: url("https://news.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/UAM_7277-1024x576.jpg"); }
								</style>
								<div class="image-inner-container">
								</div>
							</div>
							<div class="col-12 col-md-6 text-container">
								<div class="text-inner-container">
									<h2 class="h3">
										<span class="category"><a href="" aria-label="See more  stories" data-moduletitle="Patient Stories" data-categorytitle=""></a></span><span class="sr-only">: </span>										<span class="title">Myeloma Patient, Family Credit UAMS for His Improved Outlook</span>
									</h2>
									<p><p>Chris Seab has come a long way since his myeloma diagnosis, and the future looks better every day.</p>
<div></div></p>
									<div class="cta-container">
										<a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://news.uams.edu/2026/09/04/myeloma-patient-family-credit-uams-for-his-improved-outlook/" aria-label="Read Myeloma Patient, Family Credit UAMS for His Improved Outlook" data-moduletitle="Patient Stories" data-categorytitle="">Read more</a>
